e data only because  I've researched that aspect a little bit  I mean I think earlier on in Bitcoin  when the Silk Road was shut down which  was this kind of like dark web hub for  the sale of everything from you know  assassination contracts and more  commonly drugs  there was a ver was a very small change  in the amount of Bitcoin being  transacted and that sort of implied that  maybe the the illegal uses for it were  not as big as was being mentioned but in  any case it's not really our primary  conversation here but point taken  yeah I mean the bottom line is that  there isn't that anchor with respect to  Bitcoin that there is with respect to a  currency like the US dollar or the  British Pound or the yen or a national  currency it just lacks that that  government backing that maintains the  value of the currency so the value of  the US dollar is never going to go to  zero as long as you have a government  that's able to make an enforce tax laws  the value of Bitcoin could theoretically  go to zero there's nothing to prevent  that from happening  what would your feelings about its  viability change  if it was more widely accepted not  necessarily by governments but by all  but by people in other words if the  number of people in the market cap of it  continued to increase and it did start  to be accepted as a form of payment  would that change your mind on it or is  it based on the fundamentals of its  structure that you're that you're  feeling this way I don't know that I  have my mind made up on it in some  respects so I think it's an a  fascinating thing that we're all getting  to watch you know what's happening with  these crypto currencies in bit point you  can Bitcoin in particular and to the  extent that it actually begins to  function in the ways that early on you  know people said it was going to be an  important means of exchange we're not  really seeing that but I think if we do  begin to see that happening it's going  to be fascinating to watch and yeah I  can I can definitely change my  perspective of the usefulness of this  cryptocurrency and others like it as we  watch this experiment unfold I just  don't have really strong opinions on it  one way or another yet to circle back  more specifically to at least the US  government and its relationship to debt  deficit and the ideas of modern monetary  theory is there any particular policy  sort of to round out our conversation  that you think that if we could get  wholesale sort of buy-in and  understanding of the principles of mmt  there was one policy that it would point  to our legislators enacting that would  most benefit society is there something  you have in mind I think probably the  one that I would go to is the federal  job guarantee I mean this is the this is  the kind of cornerstone policy in a  sense with the MMT school of thought the  basic idea here is that there's no other  economic school of thought that has a  solution for the problem of involuntary  unemployment not a single one and so the  best that you can do in any of the  mainstream approaches to economics is to  say to the central bank you know target  the unemployment rate keep the level of  unemployment at just the right level so  that we prevent inflation from  accelerating above 2% per year in other  words were  lying on policymakers to maintain a  certain amount of unemployment in the  system as a guard against inflation and  what mmt does is say that's  extraordinarily inefficient  it's very wasteful there are costs borne  not just you know in terms of lost  output but a variety of social costs as  well that the economy would function  better that we would have a more  prosperous economy if we  institutionalize full employment and the  way to do that is through a federally  funded but locally administered jobs  program and it would just make the  overall economy function better because  what it does is it takes the automatic  stabilizers the features that are  already there today to 